WEBVTT

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.360
Two identical GPUs simplify driver features, memory balance, expected speed, replacement,

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.360
and split planning.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.175
They do not eliminate communication overhead, slot, lane, power, cooling,

00:00:10.175 --> 00:00:11.480
or runtime requirements.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.370
Mixed NVIDIA GPUs may be practical when reusing hardware,

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:18.400
but different memory sizes and compute capabilities complicate balanced splits.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.130
The faster card may wait for the slower one,

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.027
while the smaller card limits some strategies.

00:00:22.027 --> 00:00:26.800
A GPU plus CPU fallback can fit a model economically when interactive speed is not

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:27.570
critical.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.395
It should be benchmarked with realistic context because CPU-resident work and memory

00:00:31.395 --> 00:00:33.205
transfer may dominate generation.

00:00:33.205 --> 00:00:37.720
A workstation tower centralizes storage and management but concentrates heat, noise,

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:38.720
power, and failure.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.240
Used datacenter GPUs can offer memory value yet may require specialized cooling, power,

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.405
drivers, brackets, or display arrangements.

00:00:45.405 --> 00:00:48.810
External GPU experiments are useful for learning but add enclosure and interface

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:49.446
constraints.

00:00:49.446 --> 00:00:54.415
A production design should not assume an experimental link offers desktop-slot behavior or

00:00:54.415 --> 00:00:55.280
support.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.415
Multiple single-GPU servers can isolate models, users, failures, and upgrades.

00:00:59.415 --> 00:01:02.320
Networking and orchestration remain, but each node is simpler.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.990
Separate machines are often better when the goal is parallel workloads rather than one

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:06.782
split model.

00:01:06.782 --> 00:01:10.300
A scheduler should understand model residency, memory headroom, queue length,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.023
request type, and device health.

00:01:12.023 --> 00:01:16.645
Round-robin routing alone can overload a smaller GPU or repeatedly trigger expensive model

00:01:16.645 --> 00:01:16.990
swaps.
